// RESOLVED (Eric's ruling 2026-08-13 + root cause): a reference to a
// bottomed binding is NOT itself bottom — and the ByRef machinery
// never said it was. The real bug was interp-side in the unary ops:
// Neg (and Not) re-minted FRESH_BOTTOM unconditionally from any
// bottomed operand, turning a quiet STANDING bottom into a phantom
// triggering event every cycle; the enclosing Bind's triggering-only
// publish then stomped the externally written value each cycle, so
// the write could never surface. Fixed to the binop join idiom
// (fresh iff the delivery triggered, else ride). The bare
// ByRef-of-bottom shape (no neg) always agreed.
// aug13c hz0 generate/000006: ByRef of a BOTTOMED bind — the write
// never lands in the interp, lands in the kernel.
// v2's producer is -(0/0) (bottom); v5 = &v2 while v2 is bottom; the
// ByRef production presumably bottoms with it, so *v5 <- 0 has no
// resolved target and the write is dropped — v2 stays bottom and the
// program is silent forever (interp Trace([])). The kernel resolves
// the BindId statically, the write lands, and v2 % -1000 emits 0
// (jit Trace([1:0])).
// ADJUDICATE: is a reference to a bottomed BINDING itself bottom
// (the reference is to the binding, not the value — arguably it
// should always be valid)? Adjacent to refwrite-guard-flip-aug2026
// (aug08e) and the net::write path-switch flag. Whichever engine is
// ruled right, the other needs the fix at the ByRef production /
// static-resolve seam.
{let v1 = 'a: Number|v0: 'a| -> 'a v0 - v0; let v2 = -((cast<i64>(u8:0)$ / v1(i64:0))); let v5 = &v2; *v5 <- i64:0; v2 % cast<i64>(z32:-1000)$}
